and we are joined now by Senator Tim  Scott of South Carolina he is the only  black Republican in the Senate good  morning senator Scott I wanted to start  beneath all the smoke over the weekend  our calls for systemic change but the  White House National Security Advisor  Robert O'Brien over the weekend said I  don't think there's systemic racism  99.9% of our law enforcement officials  are great Americans he said you yourself  have described being stopped seven times  in a year do you think there's systemic  racism and how should we address it  well there's no doubt that our nation  the original sin was that of racism this  is a reason why in the Constitution I  was 3/5 of a man the question is what's  changed since then we've made a lot of  progress but let's just be clear there's  a lot of progress still to be made one  of the reasons why I've encouraged the  White House to take a look at having the  Department of Justice focus on race and  justice it's because it's one of the  areas where Americans want fairness if  there's a place in this nation that we  need to have leaps in the right  direction it's in our justice system and  fairness one of the things we all  celebrated Republicans and Democrats  blacks and whites was the criminal  justice reform package that was passed a  couple of years ago because there was an  unfair application of the law in  sentencing and we wanted to see that  corrected uhaah you saw people coming  together Cory Booker myself the  president Van Jones all working towards  the same outcome if we took that same  energy and focus first on mr. Floyd is  dead because the officer murdered him  mr. Oberoi is dead because two guys in a  pickup truck shot him in the middle of  the street at 1:00 p.m. for someone to  suggest that aren't racial challenges  and patterns is for someone to be blind  and so we cannot deny the truth but we  at the same time I just said this  recently we need to address this issue  this very difficult issue by recognizing  that you can both make progress and have  parts of it look like regression we have  to see both sides of the picture it's  not a a binary choice is the choice of  frog  and sometimes there's a regressive state  we can make them both work better better  if we're focused but senator if if  you're not acknowledging the problem as  the National Security Advisor does not  seem to be how do you change something  you're not even acknowledging well  there's about 340 million nine hundred  ninety nine thousand other Americans  that we can all work together to do so  the fact that one person doesn't see it  the way that it should be seen that it's  clear and evident for me when I walk  into the Capitol I'm stopped  even with my Senate pin on because I'm  an African American they didn't believe  my pin was mine I I get it but I'm not  going to focus my attention in the midst  of this challenge on one individual who  got it wrong I want to focus my  attention on folks like John Lewis that  peaceful protesting led to systemic  change in our nation if all we do is  celebrate the negative and frankly I  know it sells better we'll miss the very  important point the essence of the point  is every single American wants to  understand their own intrinsic value and  want to allow that to be expressed  publicly when you can't go for a jog as  an african-american man that that  questions whether or not your intrinsic  value is on par with every other  American so we need to wrestle with  these very significant issues for those  who believe that racism is dead and gone  and that 99.9% of any group as fewer  they're just living in a different  universe I will say that the vast  majority of law enforcement officers are  good but we need to deal with the ones  that are not as well senator you've  spoken with the president you told him  that you were reportedly that you  thought his first tweets calling some of  the protesters thugs and that the  looting when the looting starts the  shooting starts was not constructive he  has since offered condolences to the  family but he has not discussed solving  a systemic problem do you want him to  address that has he indicated to you he  will well the president I didn't have a  constructive conversation on Saturday I  thought he was very much in a listening  mode and I thank him for that we did  talk about next steps going forward  certainly I suggested that it would be  good for us  to have a conversation with some of the  leading voices in the african-american  community and some of the leading voices  in the law-enforcement community without  a bunch of cameras though I want a real  raw conversation where people get to  understand and appreciate the other's  perspective I did that with congressman  trey Gowdy in South Carolina after the  Walter Scott shooting after the church  shooting I wanted to bring communities  of color together with law enforcement  so that we could actually have a  conversation without cameras it  literally led to law enforcement  officers and pastors doing ride-alongs  and having a raw conversation I never  forget a black pastor who said I'm  afraid for my life but I'm more afraid  for the life of my child when he's  driving down the road that's a serious  challenge do you think very quickly  senator do you think the president sees  that there's a systemic problem as you  do I do think he does I talking to the  president he said very clearly with the  actions in Minnesota was terrible and he  even questioned of what is going on with  the other three officers so when we have  a president who's willing to listen  we're in a better position I hope that  over the next seven days you'll see some  skin on the bones of some of the  recommendations I have a piece of  legislation called the Walter Scott  notification act that would help us  understand the police shootings around  the country there's no one place you can  go to get the information today so I'm  hoping that we'll see that and other  pieces of legislation going forward all  right senator Scott thank you so much  for being with us this morning
 

